U2B = {}

U2B[31] = {
"q": "State any two HRD subsystems.",
"intro": """A subsystem is a part of the HRD system with its own work, contributing to the larger process of developing people and climate. For 8 marks, name two subsystems clearly, explain each as if the reader has never heard of them, give an example, and then briefly name the others and show a link. Good pairs: performance appraisal and training; or potential appraisal and career planning; or feedback/counselling and training.""",
"pts": [
("First subsystem — performance appraisal",
"""Performance appraisal is a planned review of how well an employee has done against agreed work and behaviour. In HRD it is not only a mark for salary. It includes goal setting, review, two-way feedback, and an individual development plan. Example: a teacher and the principal agree on teaching quality and student support goals in June, review them in March, and decide that the teacher will attend a classroom-management workshop and be coached for one term. That is appraisal as HRD."""),
("Why appraisal is a subsystem, not a full HRD system",
"""It diagnoses and plans, but it does not by itself create skill. Training, career moves and daily coaching must follow. If the organisation only fills appraisal forms, employees will correctly say HRD is missing."""),
("Second subsystem — training",
"""Training is a planned effort to help employees learn job-related knowledge, skills and attitudes. It may be on-the-job, off-the-job, or e-learning. Example: new hospital technicians practise on a dummy before they touch patients, then work under a senior. Training contributes to development when it is based on a real need from appraisal or TNA and when the workplace allows use of the skill."""),
("Why training is only a subsystem",
"""The notes and the whole HRD idea warn that training is not the answer to all problems and not the whole of HRD. Climate, counselling and career must support it. Happy classrooms with no job change mean training is floating, not functioning as a subsystem."""),
("Other subsystems you should mention",
"""Potential appraisal (future capacity), career planning (paths), feedback and counselling (conversation that changes people), organisation development and climate mechanisms (teams and culture). Naming them shows you know the map."""),
("A simple interrelation example",
"""Appraisal finds weak listening → counselling explores whether it is skill or fear → training builds the skill → career plan uses better listening in a customer-facing role → next appraisal checks behaviour. Two named subsystems live inside this cycle."""),
("Mistakes to avoid in the exam",
"""Do not write only two words. Do not define HRM instead of HRD. Do not say training and HRD are the same. Do not forget an example."""),
("How to fill 8 marks",
"""Introduction 8 lines. Subsystem 1: meaning, HRD use, example (1/3 page). Subsystem 2: same. Other subsystems + one link paragraph. Conclusion."""),
],
"conc": """Two standard subsystems to name are performance appraisal and training. Explain each fully with an example, list the remaining subsystems, and show that they work as parts of one HRD system. Names alone cannot earn 8 marks."""
}

U2B[32] = {
"q": "State any two purposes of feedback in HRD practices.",
"intro": """Feedback in HRD is specific information given to a person (or by a person upward to the organisation) about behaviour or results, so that learning can happen. It is not scolding, and it is not vague praise. For 8 marks, state two main purposes in depth, then add other purposes so the answer is complete.""",
"pts": [
("First purpose — to improve current performance",
"""The employee needs to know where he or she stands against expectations in time to correct. A cook told the same day that the gravy is too salty can change tonight’s food. A yearly remark ‘food average’ cannot. This purpose is corrective. Without it, errors repeat and customers suffer. In HRD language, feedback reduces the gap between standard and actual when the person can control that gap."""),
("How this purpose is carried out",
"""Immediate, behaviour-focused comments by the supervisor; mid-period reviews; quality checklists discussed with the worker. Tone matters: respect keeps the person open. Humiliation makes the person defend, not improve — then this purpose fails even if the information was correct."""),
("Second purpose — to support learning and long-term development",
"""Feedback is how people learn from work itself, which the notes call informal HRD (coaching by a manager). It reinforces what is going well so that good behaviour continues, and it guides training, stretch jobs and career choices. Example: ‘You explain products clearly; you still freeze when the customer is angry’ points both to a strength to use and to a skill to train. Development becomes targeted."""),
("Motivation as a related purpose",
"""Recognition through honest positive feedback meets higher-order needs — achievement and esteem — which HRD stresses compared with only monetary motivation in HRM. People repeat what is noticed."""),
("Role clarification",
"""Feedback often reveals that the employee and the boss did not share the same picture of the job. Clarifying the role develops the dyad, an HRD system goal."""),
("Climate-building purpose",
"""Open, respectful, two-way feedback signals trust and openness (HRD climate). Absent or only negative feedback signals fear. So feedback is both a tool and a climate message."""),
("System improvement and evaluation",
"""Upward feedback tells the organisation about broken processes and poor support. Feedback after training (reaction, behaviour) improves HRD design. These are purposes beyond the single employee."""),
("Exam caution",
"""Write two purposes as long paragraphs with examples, then a cluster of other purposes. Do not define feedback in one line and stop."""),
],
"conc": """Two central purposes of feedback in HRD are (1) to improve present performance through timely, specific information, and (2) to support learning and development by reinforcing strengths and guiding next growth steps. Motivation, role clarity, climate and system improvement are additional purposes. Feedback is information for growth, not insult."""
}

U2B[33] = {
"q": "Analyse how poor coordination among HRD subsystems can reduce the effectiveness of employee development.",
"intro": """Poor coordination means each HRD subsystem works in its own silo: appraisal does not talk to training, training does not talk to career, potential labels do not change promotions, counselling never happens after the rating. Effectiveness of employee development means people actually become more capable, more ready for future roles, and more able to work together. Analysis must show broken links → contradictory messages → wasted effort → low development, and then a vicious cycle of lost credibility.""",
"pts": [
("Appraisal not feeding training",
"""Employees are rated weak on a skill year after year but never trained or coached. They conclude the organisation is not serious. The gap remains. Development effectiveness is zero for that skill despite ‘complete’ appraisal files."""),
("Training unrelated to career and work",
"""People attend courses that do not lead to any new responsibility or even to a chance to practise. Motivation to learn falls. Transfer fails. Development looks busy (attendance) and is ineffective (no capability on the job)."""),
("Potential ignored when promotions happen",
"""HRD says someone is high potential; the actual promotion goes by seniority or politics. High-potential staff leave. Others see HRD as a lie. Effectiveness of talent development collapses because the most important career actions ignore HRD data."""),
("No counselling after appraisal",
"""Numbers are written; no conversation, no emotional support, no joint plan. Data without insight. People who needed help with stress or role confusion stay stuck. Development is not only cognitive; counselling’s absence reduces effectiveness for whole-person growth."""),
("Conflicting messages between subsystems",
"""Training teaches teamwork; appraisal and bonus honour only individual targets. Employees follow the reward. Net development of collaboration can be negative — the organisation is less developed after ‘HRD activity’ than before. This is a key analytical point."""),
("Duplication, gaps and no memory",
"""Two cells run similar programmes; some roles get nothing. Without a shared file, each year starts from zero. Inefficiency plus inequity. Development is uneven and unfair, which also violates HRD’s equity objective."""),
("Climate damage",
"""Uncoordinated HRD feels chaotic and political. Trust falls. In a weaker climate, even the remaining good practices transfer less. Poor coordination therefore reduces effectiveness both directly (broken links) and indirectly (worse soil)."""),
("Vicious cycle of credibility",
"""When development does not happen, business heads cut HRD budget. Subsystems shrink further and coordinate even less. Employee development slows again. Analysis should end with this loop so the examiner sees you understand dynamics, not only a list of problems."""),
],
"conc": """Poor coordination breaks the chain from diagnosis to learning to career to new behaviour, sends conflicting messages, wastes money, and damages climate and credibility. Employee development then becomes slow, unfair and unstrategic. Coordination is a condition of HRD effectiveness, not a luxury."""
}

U2B[34] = {
"q": "Analyse how HRD practices can be aligned with organisational strategy in a rapidly changing organisation.",
"intro": """A rapidly changing organisation faces new technology, markets, structures or competition every year, sometimes every quarter. Strategy itself is revised often. Alignment cannot mean a five-year frozen training list. Analysis should show how HRD practices — TNA, appraisal, career, training methods, OD — can stay coupled to a moving strategy. The need is agility with direction, not chaos and not rigidity.""",
"pts": [
("Continuous translation of strategy into competencies",
"""Whenever strategy shifts (new product, digital channel, new geography), HRD and business heads quickly update ‘what people must be able to do’. Waiting for the next printed handbook is misalignment. This translation meeting is the core alignment practice."""),
("Shorter diagnosis cycles",
"""Pulse TNA, short climate checks, and looking at error data quarterly rather than a giant study every three years. Fast change creates new gaps quickly; slow diagnosis trains yesterday’s need."""),
("Flexible learning methods",
"""Blended, just-in-time, on-the-job, e-learning and short workshops beat only long residential courses. People cannot leave a crisis workplace for two weeks every time the software changes. Method flexibility is how alignment stays practical."""),
("Organisation development, not only skill training",
"""Rapid change is also emotional and structural: new teams, mergers, lost roles. HRD must facilitate change, counselling and new collaboration. Alignment includes helping people through the change that strategy created."""),
("Career systems that allow movement",
"""Rigid ladders assume a stable pyramid. In rapid change, projects, lateral moves and temporary leadership roles develop people faster. Career planning remains, but paths are reviewed more often (notes already say career planning is continuous because environment changes)."""),
("Line managers and HRD in the same business review",
"""Alignment is a conversation rhythm: ‘here is the market shift; here is the capability risk; here is the development action this quarter.’ If HRD is not in the room, practices drift."""),
("Stopping obsolete practices",
"""Alignment is also subtraction. Drop appraisal items and courses that belong to yesterday’s strategy so that people are not pulled in two directions. Analysis: adding without deleting is false alignment."""),
("New measures of HRD success",
"""In a fast context, judge HRD by speed of capability building and change readiness, not only by number of programmes. What you measure is what aligns. Evaluation must match the strategy’s time pressure."""),
],
"conc": """In a rapidly changing organisation, alignment is a repeating loop: translate strategy → diagnose quickly → learn flexibly → support change → review career and drop obsolete practices. Write methods, not slogans. Frozen HRD cannot serve a moving strategy."""
}

U2B[35] = {
"q": "Evaluate the effectiveness of using potential appraisal together with performance appraisal for employee development.",
"intro": """Evaluate means compare combined use with using either tool alone, state benefits, state risks, give conditions for success, and end with a verdict. Performance appraisal asks how well the present job was done. Potential appraisal asks how far the person can go in other or higher work. Employee development needs both yesterday’s evidence and tomorrow’s capacity.""",
"pts": [
("They answer different development questions",
"""A person can be excellent today and still lack capacity for a larger leadership role. A person can be average in a badly fitting job and still have high potential elsewhere. One number cannot answer both questions. Combined use is more effective because it matches Unit 1’s dual objective: present role and future role."""),
("Avoids wrong promotions — a strong effectiveness argument",
"""Promoting the best salesman to sales manager only because of performance is a classic failure (Peter Principle). Combined use protects the person from a role they cannot do and protects the organisation from losing a good specialist and gaining a bad manager."""),
("Richer, fairer development paths",
"""High performance + high potential → leadership track with stretch jobs. High performance + limited managerial potential → specialist excellence, education, recognition without a forced ladder. Low performance + high potential → coaching and perhaps role change. Low both → basic skill or exit decisions. Development becomes tailored, which is more effective than one path for all."""),
("Motivation and transparency when done well",
"""Employees understand why some get stretch roles. Criteria are dual, not mysterious. That fairness supports HRD climate and willingness to develop."""),
("Risks that reduce effectiveness (must be in an evaluation)",
"""Secret potential lists; bias; both judgements made in five minutes by one untrained boss; no feedback to the employee; no career action after the label. Then combined use is political and can harm development more than performance-only appraisal."""),
("Conditions for effectiveness",
"""Separate time and tools for the two judgements; trained assessors; multiple inputs; feedback; training and career moves that actually follow; review of potential as people grow. Effectiveness is not automatic from using two forms."""),
("Compared with potential-only or performance-only",
"""Performance-only HRD over-invests in today’s job. Potential-only HRD may ignore that the person is failing customers today. Together they balance current contribution and future capability."""),
("Verdict",
"""Using potential appraisal together with performance appraisal is highly effective for employee development when the two are methodologically separated, fair, transparent to the employee, and followed by real development actions. Without those conditions, the combination is ineffective or damaging. Recommend combined use with quality, not combined use as paperwork."""),
],
"conc": """Give a clear evaluation: together is better than either alone, because present and future development both matter — provided quality, fairness and follow-up exist. Do not praise potential appraisal blindly."""
}

U2B[36] = {
"q": "Analyse how an HRD system can become ineffective when its practices are designed without organisational strategy alignment.",
"intro": """Ineffectiveness here means the HRD system may look active — forms, courses, speeches — yet people do not gain the capabilities the organisation needs, and business results and future readiness do not improve. Analysis traces how missing alignment produces wrong content, broken promises, cynicism and finally strategic failure.""",
"pts": [
("Wrong competencies are developed",
"""Staff become very good at yesterday’s work while strategy needs new capabilities (digital, new market, new quality). Effort is large; direction is wrong. This is ineffectiveness despite hard work."""),
("Budget is spent on the periphery",
"""Popular programmes flourish; critical roles for the strategy stay weak. Opportunity cost is the centre of the analysis. A full training calendar can hide empty strategy support."""),
("Career paths become promises to nowhere",
"""Plans that do not match the future structure (for example, many managerial rungs while the firm is flattening) create anger when jobs never appear. Climate and trust fall, which further reduces HRD effectiveness."""),
("Appraisal chases old measures",
"""People ‘perform’ on a scorecard that strategy has already abandoned (volume without quality, for example). HRD then reinforces the wrong behaviour. The system is worse than inactive — it is actively misaligned."""),
("Top management withdraws support",
"""Business heads see no return and cut HRD. The system shrinks. Remaining practices become even more ritual. Ineffectiveness becomes structural."""),
("Talent leaves; who stays may be less capable",
"""High performers go to firms where growth matches business. HRD then works with a weaker audience. Capability gap versus strategy widens."""),
("Strategy implementation itself fails",
"""The organisation cannot execute its own plan. HRD ineffectiveness becomes organisational ineffectiveness — quality failure, digital lag, failed expansion. Analysis must connect the two."""),
("Illusion of success delays correction",
"""High training days and completed appraisals look good in reports until a crisis. Activity metrics hide irrelevance. An unaligned system can therefore remain ‘successful’ on paper while being ineffective in reality for a long time."""),
],
"conc": """Without strategy alignment, HRD develops the wrong things, wastes money, breaks career trust, teaches old behaviours, loses support and talent, and finally fails the business. Alignment is a condition of effectiveness, not a decoration on an HRD brochure."""
}

U2B[37] = {
"q": "State any two benefits of integrating career planning with potential appraisal.",
"intro": """Integration means career paths are based on a serious judgement of potential, and potential labels are followed by real career actions (rotation, training, succession), not by a secret stamp in a file. For 8 marks, explain two benefits fully, then add others.""",
"pts": [
("First benefit — realistic career paths and less frustration",
"""Employees are not promised impossible ladders. A strong specialist is not told ‘you will be GM in three years’ if potential appraisal shows limited interest or skill in managing people. Counselling can be truthful. Frustration, later feelings of betrayal, and inner resignation fall. This is a human benefit and a climate benefit."""),
("Why realism is an HRD benefit",
"""Notes say career planning should identify match and mismatch between aspirations and opportunities. Potential appraisal supplies evidence for that match. Integration makes the career-planning process honest, which is developmental because the person can plan education and effort that will actually pay."""),
("Second benefit — better utilisation of talent",
"""High-potential employees get timely stretch roles, mentoring and training instead of waiting behind seniority only. The organisation uses its future resource while the person is still motivated. Key jobs have prepared people (succession). Development money goes where potential and organisational need meet."""),
("Example",
"""Two engineers perform equally well. Potential appraisal shows one has people leadership and conceptual range; the other is a deep designer. Integrated career planning sends the first through project-lead roles and the second through a specialist fellowship. Both stay; both grow; the firm is not forced to ruin a designer by making him a reluctant boss."""),
("Additional benefit — fewer wrong promotions",
"""Potential qualifies career moves beyond current performance. Customer and team damage from unfit managers is reduced."""),
("Additional benefit — motivation and retention",
"""Visible, fair growth paths keep capable people. Notes list attracting and retaining the right persons and reducing turnover as career-planning aims. Integration makes those aims believable."""),
("Additional benefit — better counselling quality",
"""Advice is evidence-based rather than generic ‘work hard and you will rise’. Employees can set achievable goals (an aim of counselling in the notes)."""),
("Exam wrap",
"""Two headline benefits as long paragraphs, then succession, targeted investment, fewer wrong promotions, retention. Conclusion: both the individual and the organisation gain when career and potential talk to each other."""),
],
"conc": """Two main benefits of integration are (1) realistic career paths that reduce false hope and (2) better, timely utilisation of high-potential talent. Other benefits include succession, fairer promotions, retention and better counselling. Integration turns labels into development."""
}

U2B[38] = {
"q": "Mention any two features of an effective HRD practice.",
"intro": """A feature is a quality you would look for if asked, ‘Is this appraisal / training / career scheme effective as HRD?’ Effective means it actually helps the development process rather than hindering it with fear or paperwork. Name two features in depth, then list others.""",
"pts": [
("First feature — developmental purpose in real operation",
"""The practice exists to help people learn and grow, not only to control, rank, or complete a legal file. You can see this in what happens at the end: an individual development plan, coaching, a chance to practise. Example: an appraisal discussion that spends time on ‘what will you try next quarter’ is developmental. A five-minute form-signing for increment is not, even if the form heading says HRD."""),
("How to test this feature",
"""Ask employees whether anything in their work changed after the practice. If never, the purpose is not developmental in reality. T.V. Rao’s test: does the mechanism promote or hinder the HRD process?"""),
("Second feature — integration with other subsystems and with strategy",
"""An effective practice is joined to the rest of HRD and to organisational goals. Training comes from appraisal gaps and career plans and strategy, not from a vendor’s catalogue. Career talks use potential data. Feedback is not a separate annual picnic. Isolation is a feature of ineffective practice."""),
("Example of integration as a visible feature",
"""After a quality-training programme, the next appraisal includes a quality behaviour, the boss coaches weekly, and a project uses the new method. That training practice is effective because it is linked."""),
("Other features to mention",
"""Need-based (diagnosis, TNA). Line-manager involvement. Fairness and transparency. Top-management support and resources. Consistency with HRD climate (does not terrify). Evaluated and improved. Culture-sensitive design."""),
("Fairness as a strong extra feature",
"""Equity of access — not only favourites developed — is both an HRD objective and a feature of effectiveness, because unfair practices destroy climate and then nothing transfers."""),
("Evaluation as a feature",
"""Effective practices have a review: climate survey, Kirkpatrick levels, HRD audit. Practices that never look at impact rot."""),
("Exam shape",
"""Two features as full explanations with ‘how you would recognise them’, then a cluster of other features. Do not write two adjectives (‘good’, ‘useful’) without meaning."""),
],
"conc": """Two key features of an effective HRD practice are a genuine developmental purpose (growth actions follow) and integration with other HRD subsystems and organisational strategy. Add diagnosis, line ownership, fairness, support, climate fit and evaluation. Features are what you can observe, not what the brochure claims."""
}

U2B[39] = {
"q": "Design an integrated HRD system for an organisation that wants to connect performance, potential, training, career planning, feedback, and counselling.",
"intro": """Design means propose structure, sequence, roles and checks. The organisation wants six named elements to work as one system. Do not write six separate mini-essays. Draw (in words) one annual cycle on a shared language of competencies, with line managers as owners and HRD as facilitator.""",
"pts": [
("Policy architecture and roles",
"""One HRD policy: these six elements form a single cycle; every line manager develops his or her people; HRD designs tools, trains managers, and evaluates; a small talent/HRD committee of business heads meets twice a year to align with strategy. Top management signs and funds this."""),
("Common language and file (the spine)",
"""One competency framework for roles, and one employee development file holding goals, appraisals, potential notes, IDP, training history, career talk notes. Without this spine, ‘integration’ is a slogan because nobody can see the connections."""),
("The annual cycle (sequence of the six)",
"""(1) Joint goal setting. (2) Ongoing feedback during the year. (3) Performance appraisal. (4) Counselling conversation and written IDP. (5) Potential review where relevant. (6) Career discussion. (7) Training and job-experience plan from the IDP. (8) On-the-job support. (9) Evaluation of learning and behaviour. Then the next year’s goals. Write this sequence clearly; it is the design."""),
("Feedback and counselling design in detail",
"""Quarterly check-ins plus the appraisal counselling. Managers trained in listening and in keeping criticism on performance. Optional professional counsellor for difficult cases. Confidentiality rules written. Follow-up dates in the IDP."""),
("How training connects",
"""IDPs are aggregated into a training calendar. Group programmes still go through TNA. Bosses receive a transfer brief: what to observe after the course. Unused training is reported back to the committee."""),
("How potential and career connect",
"""A talent council uses potential data for rotations and succession, then talks to the employee — no forever-secret list without development. Career paths include specialist as well as managerial routes. Reviews when strategy changes."""),
("Climate so that data are honest",
"""Openness and fairness of access. If people fear the cycle, they will fake every form and integration will process lies. Climate work (communication, no humiliation of ratings) is part of the design."""),
("Audit of connections",
"""Once a year sample: did IDP actions happen? Did training match appraisal gaps? Did high-potential people get stretch roles? Ask employees whether the six felt connected. Repair broken links. This audit is T.V. Rao’s ‘examine whether mechanisms help or hinder’ made practical."""),
],
"conc": """Your design is one policy, one competency spine, one annual cycle that sequences performance, feedback, counselling, potential, career and training, plus climate and an audit of links. That is an integrated HRD system, not six parallel departments."""
}

U2B[40] = {
"q": "Evaluate a proposed HRD practice that has strong training content but weak linkage with organisational strategy, performance appraisal, and career planning.",
"intro": """Evaluate the proposal: strengths, weaknesses, likely results using Kirkpatrick’s way of thinking, a verdict, and what to change. Do not reject good teaching quality. Do reject isolation. Strong content means sound objectives inside the classroom, expert trainers, useful material. Weak linkage means the topic may not be what strategy needs, bosses do not put the skill into appraisal, and the course does not count toward any career path.""",
"pts": [
("Strength — learning inside the room may still happen",
"""Good pedagogy can produce high participant reaction and some real learning (Kirkpatrick levels 1 and 2). People may understand concepts they did not know. This strength is real and should be kept if the topic can be aligned."""),
("Weakness — strategy irrelevance",
"""The organisation may be teaching a fashionable topic while critical capabilities for the next two years are ignored. Opportunity cost: seats and rupees used on the wrong need. As HRD, the practice is then ineffective even if the class is excellent."""),
("Weakness — appraisal disconnect and failed transfer",
"""If bosses do not expect or reinforce the new behaviour, employees return to old key result areas. Level 3 (behaviour on the job) fails. Strong content cannot jump this gap alone. This is the most common failure of ‘good training’."""),
("Weakness — career disconnect and weak motivation to apply",
"""If learning does not count toward growth, only highly self-driven staff will practise. Others treat the course as a break. Development impact stays individual and accidental, not organisational."""),
("Likely results (evaluation using levels)",
"""Level 1 possibly high. Level 2 mixed to good. Level 3 poor. Level 4 (organisational results) poor. A manager who looks only at smile sheets will wrongly approve the practice."""),
("Climate signal",
"""Repeated isolated excellent courses create ‘holiday training’ cynicism. Trust in HRD falls, which later harms even well-linked programmes."""),
("Conditional value",
"""The practice may still help as general education for a few motivated employees. That is not enough to accept it as an organisational HRD practice in its present form."""),
("Verdict and redesign",
"""Do not accept as-is. Keep the strong content only after: (a) remapping the topic to strategy and TNA, (b) inserting pre- and post-appraisal goals and boss coaching, (c) placing the programme on competency and career maps, (d) evaluating behaviour and results, not only reaction. Then the same content can become effective HRD."""),
],
"conc": """Judgement: content quality is necessary but not sufficient. Without links to strategy, performance appraisal and career planning, the proposal is an expensive learning event, not an HRD practice. Recommend redesign of linkages, not celebration of the trainer alone."""
}
